(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-external-middle-ear-vignettes"]={"title":"External and Middle Ear Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 58-year-old man has recurrent wax blockage. He cleans both ears with cotton swabs every morning. What advice should he be given?","choices":["Stop putting anything into the canal","Clean twice daily instead of once","Use a swab only after showering","Use a candle to draw the wax out"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cleaning attempts are the commonest cause of impaction. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 14-month-old boy has acute otitis media. His parents declined all childhood vaccinations. How does that change the assessment?","choices":["Pseudomonas becomes the likely organism","The illness is now certainly viral","No organism changes; only the severity does","Haemophilus influenzae becomes a greater concern"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vaccination is why that organism has become less common. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 3-year-old girl with a bulging red drum, fever of 39 degrees Celsius and three days of worsening pain is judged to need antibacterial treatment. Which agent is used?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Ciprofloxacin","Metronidazole","Fluconazole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy has had a middle ear effusion for four months and his speech is behind that of his peers. What should his parents be told is the main concern?","choices":["Loss of balance","Delay in speech and language development","Permanent damage to the eardrum","Spread of infection to the brain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Months of muffled hearing during language acquisition is the real risk. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has just had an auricular haematoma drained. Which measure prevents it re-accumulating?","choices":["Twice-daily antifungal drops","A hearing aid","Ear splinting","A week of bed rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — bolsters, moulds, putty or thermoplastic splints. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 71-year-old immunocompromised man has confirmed necrotizing external otitis. Which treatment is required?","choices":["A topical antifungal","An oral antiviral","A corticosteroid alone","An antipseudomonal antibiotic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — ciprofloxacin is the named example. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman is examined for ear fullness. The clinician attaches a bulb to the otoscope and puffs air against the drum. What is being assessed?","choices":["Whether the drum moves","Whether hearing is normal","Whether the canal is patent","Whether the facial nerve works"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reduced mobility signals fluid or pressure behind it. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has a normal ear examination. Which description matches his tympanic membrane?","choices":["Blue-black and immobile","Pearly grey and mobile","Red and bulging","Retracted and immobile"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has two weeks of intense itching and mild discomfort in the right ear after using antibiotic drops. The canal contains grey-black specks and filaments described as looking like wet newspaper. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial otitis externa","Cholesteatoma","Necrotizing external otitis","Otomycosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the appearance and the itch-dominant history. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man has severe right ear pain and muffled hearing that began during the descent of a flight. The right drum is dark blue-black and barely moves on insufflation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cholesteatoma","Glomus tumour","Otitis externa","Barotrauma with haemotympanum (blood behind the eardrum)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the appearance and the pressure history together. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl is seen for a school physical. She has no complaints. The right drum is dull with a visible line of fluid behind it and moves poorly on insufflation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Barotrauma","Otitis media with effusion","Chronic otitis media","Cholesteatoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is frequently asymptomatic and found incidentally. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 15-year-old swimmer starting treatment for otitis externa asks what he should do at home. What is the correct advice?","choices":["Stop the drops as soon as the pain goes","Clean the canal daily with swabs","Swim only in chlorinated pools","Keep the ear dry and finish the full course"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the pain settles well before the infection does. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"An 8-year-old girl finished treatment for acute otitis media three weeks ago. Her drum is no longer red but is dull with fluid behind it and she still cannot hear well. What explains this?","choices":["A cholesteatoma has formed","An effusion persisting after the infection resolved","The infection has failed to respond to treatment","A new bacterial infection has begun"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a well-described sequel. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has soft wax filling the outer canal and an intact drum. He is comfortable and cooperative. Which removal method suits this situation?","choices":["Curette removal","Immediate surgical referral","Leaving it and repeating drops for six months","Forceful irrigation at high pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — soft wax and a cooperative patient. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has a dry, non-healing perforation of the right drum with no active infection. What is the appropriate step in primary care?","choices":["Irrigate the canal to clear debris","Prescribe two weeks of oral antibiotics","Reassure and take no action","Refer to an ear, nose and throat specialist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 21-year-old boxer asks what happens if a swollen ear is left undrained. What should he be told?","choices":["Permanent deformity, known as cauliflower ear","Long-term dizziness","Weakness of the face","Development of a middle ear tumour"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 33-year-old cyclist arrives with part of the auricle avulsed. A bystander has brought the detached tissue. What should be done with it?","choices":["Freeze it for grafting in six weeks","Return it to the patient to keep","Attempt reattachment","Discard it as non-viable"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — reattachment of recovered tissue is often successful. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man is told his hearing loss comes from bone growing around one of the small ear bones. Which bone is affected?","choices":["The cochlea","The stapes","The incus","The mastoid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — bony overgrowth eventually fixes it. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man has had intermittent discharge from his left ear for eight months. Otoscopy shows a hole in the drum that has never closed, with pus draining through it. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Otitis externa","Otomycosis","Chronic suppurative otitis media","Acute otitis media"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a non-healing perforation with persistent purulent drainage. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes has two weeks of severe left ear pain that is far worse than the modest redness of his canal suggests. There is copious discharge and the left side of his face is weak. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Necrotizing external otitis","Ordinary otitis externa","Acute otitis media","Otomycosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — pain out of proportion, in a diabetic, with facial nerve involvement. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 44-year-old flight attendant has had four episodes of ear barotrauma this year. What long-term option should be discussed?","choices":["Daily oral decongestants indefinitely","Routine myringotomy before each flight","Tympanostomy tubes","Giving up flying permanently"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — considered for recurrent episodes in frequent flyers. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 19-year-old wrestler has a tense, boggy swelling of the upper auricle after a match two hours ago. The normal ridges of the ear are no longer visible. What should be done?","choices":["Reassure; it will resorb","Drain it now","Apply ice and review in a week","Refer routinely to plastic surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Early drainage is what prevents permanent deformity. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has had a keloid excised from his earlobe. What should he be told about follow-up?","choices":["He must be followed closely for recurrence","No follow-up is needed once excised","He will need annual hearing tests","He should expect it to become malignant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — recurrence is common. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has mild unilateral otosclerosis and says it does not trouble him at all. What is appropriate?","choices":["Bilateral surgery in one sitting","A course of oral antibiotics","Tympanostomy tubes","Observation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — surgery is elective, and unilateral untroublesome disease may simply be watched. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has a traumatic perforation that is expected to heal on its own. What should he be told to watch for?","choices":["Mild fullness for a day","A single episode of sneezing","Worsening hearing or dizziness","A change in the colour of his wax"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — trauma can disrupt the ossicles or the inner ear as well. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has three years of gradually worsening hearing in both ears, worse on the right. She says she hears better in a noisy restaurant than in a quiet room. Both drums look completely normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Otosclerosis","Presbycusis","Otitis media with effusion","Cholesteatoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — gradual conductive loss with a normal drum, and better hearing in noise. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy develops ear pain four days into a runny nose and cough. What is the most likely underlying cause of his ear infection?","choices":["Aspergillus niger","A virus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most acute otitis media is viral, which is why most resolves without antibiotics. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman must fly tomorrow and has mild nasal congestion from a cold. She asks how to reduce her risk of ear pain. What should she be advised?","choices":["Sleep for the whole descent","Use antibiotic drops before boarding","Take a decongestant beforehand and equalise on descent","Avoid drinking anything during the flight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — swallowing, yawning and gentle exhalation against resistance all help. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 78-year-old diabetic woman is suspected of having necrotizing external otitis. Which investigation confirms it?","choices":["Tympanometry","Imaging of the skull base","A canal swab","An audiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging showing bony involvement. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has a small perforation that appeared when her acute otitis media discharged three days ago. She asks how long it will take to heal. What should she be told?","choices":["It will never close","It will close only after antibiotics","It may close within days","It will take at least a year"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — as little as forty-eight to seventy-two hours in this setting. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 47"}]};
